{"type":"message","id":"5953e821","parentId":"33f9f0ca","timestamp":"2026-10-29T02:23:32.541Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"No.\n\nMaybe the archived page's chart data can be accessed with known Pudding conventions: pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/ likely has /assets/ or /js/. Try web_fetch \"https://pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/js/main.js\". But even if we get JS, labels may be there. Let's inspect via web_view page source? Not available.\n\nTry URLs:\n- https://pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/scripts/main.js\n- https://pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/js/index.js\n- https://pudding.cool/2017/02/new-slang/assets/...\nHard.\n\nAlternative: the data folder only holds state-weekly-searches.csv. The task is designed such that some story findings are reproducible from this data. Likely the target findings are:\n1. 2016 emerging slang ranking (maybe reproducible via data?).\n2. Woke geography (top/bottom states).\n3. SJW geography (OR, VT).\n4. Boolin south.\n5. Shook California summer.\n6. Bough Mississippi.\n7. Broccoli 75% of Netflix peak.\n\nBut with per-word normalization, the broccoli 75% may not be reproducible. The ranking might not be. The state patterns might be.\n\nLet's understand how the data was normalized. Values range up to 1, with 3 decimals. Check if values look like fractions of the word's max: For each word, max=1.000. Yes. So each word normalized separately. Thus within-word state/time comparisons valid, across-word magnitude not.\n\nMaybe the data is exactly what the Pudding used for the state-level heatmaps: for each word, \"search interest\" scaled to peak = 1. The story's maps show relative interest by state over time.\n\nLet's check the data's time range: 11/30/14 to 12/11/16. 107 weeks. It includes 2015 and 2016 words. Perhaps the article's main visualization is a grid of small multiples for each word showing state-level weekly values. The 2015 words and 2016 words each have a list. The 2016 list rank could be based on \"how much it grew\" or \"peak search interest\" but normalized data can't rank across words. Unless ranking uses absolute search volume from Google Trends, not in CSV.\n\nWait, perhaps values are not normalized to each word's own max but the max being 1.0 is coincidence from rounding? No, all 35 columns max exactly 1.0 — deliberate.\n\nCould ranking be based on the week the word crossed a threshold? The list 1-15 may be ordered by prominence/absolute volume from another source. The data can't give it.\n\nLet's inspect the original project's data via Wayback? Maybe Pudding published a CSV or notebook. Search via Bing: web_fetch \"https://www.bing.com/search?q=...\" Let's try.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_xPZlNPw5nyjndeEESaBa3587","name":"web_fetch","arguments":{"url":"https://www.bing.com/search?q=%22state-weekly-searches%22+slang"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_01_Nlp58kjDSTzDiDmTiNND1690","name":"web_fetch","arguments":{"url":"https://www.bing.com/search?q=pudding.cool+%22new-slang%22+github+data"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-flash","usage":{"input":229,"output":744,"cacheRead":22912,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":23885,"cost":{"input":0.00003206,"output":0.00020832,"cacheRead":0.0000641536,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0003045336}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1793240606920,"responseId":"9c1da1a7-84dd-4d65-9435-48e8f158ceeb"}}
