{"type":"message","id":"b2f0d02c","parentId":"ec01308d","timestamp":"2026-10-29T02:23:40.498Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Search useless.\n\nLet's focus on the data. We need to determine the story's findings that the CSV can reproduce. The instruction likely generated from an actual Pudding project with a known dataset; the expected facts may be particular. Let's think of what the story charts show.\n\nThe story text says:\n- \"Using Google searches for words' definitions, I examined slang that was once rarely searched but is now relatively popular.\"\n- \"How the Kids Speak: Emerging slang, by the year it rose into popularity\" lists words by year, 2016 ranked 1-15.\n- Under broccoli: \"searches for the word's definition have climbed to about 75% of where 'Netflix and chill' peaked in 2015.\"\n- Geographic: woke in blue states; SC/AR behind; SJW in OR and VT; boolin rooted in south; shook in California this summer; bough in Mississippi.\n\nThe CSV is state-weekly searches for exactly these words. It likely underlies a set of small-multiple line charts (one per word), with state lines maybe highlighted. The geographic claims presumably derive from state averages at a particular time.\n\nLet's inspect the data columns beyond the 2015/2016 lists: chapo, spry, vape, tome, nae nae, shudder, wap, brandish, quash. These might be 2013/2014 words excluded from list? Actually the 2014 list: Fleek, Fam, Felicia, Anaconda, Slay, Yeet, Silt, Calvary, Becky, Hawking. Not these. 2013: Selfie, Bae, Turn Up, Red Nose, Emoji, Turnt, Sapiosexual, Sus. Not these. So these extra words are \"candidates\" that didn't make the final list—maybe terms that grew but not enough. The data may be the full set of words evaluated, with the list selecting some.\n\nMaybe the data's per-word weekly national trajectory can reproduce the \"emerging slang by year\" classification: e.g., 2015 words peak/rise in 2015; 2016 words rise in 2016. Indeed netflix an