{"type":"message","id":"4a0200de","parentId":"465aeb14","timestamp":"2026-10-29T02:28:53.970Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now we know the charts. The geo heatmaps can be reproduced from the CSV with 4-week moving average, and the author's annotations name states. We need to define the metric that yields these named states.\n\nLet's use the data and the chart design. The heatmap likely plots, for each state-week, the search interest normalized to the word's max, smoothed 4-week average. The state rows are ordered by census region. The annotator eyeballed \"high present-day use\" in MA, VT, NY, CT (woke) — note the story text says \"Vermont, Massachusetts, Rhode Island, New York, and California\", which differs slightly (California west coast, RI). The OCR chart says MA, VT, NY, CT plus \"northeast and west-coast\" and \"south is less woke\". The story text names RI and CA.\n\nGiven the heatmap covers 2015-2016, \"present-day use\" likely means late 2016 (right side). Let's compute 4-week moving average at the end of 2016 (Dec 2016) by state for woke and see if MA, VT, NY, CT top. We computed Sep-Dec level: DC, CA, VT, MA, MD, NY, RI, WA, CT... So CT 9, not top 4, but if we exclude DC (not a state) and look at Northeast: VT, MA, NY, CT top NE states; CA west coast. That fits \"high present-day use in MA, VT, NY, CT\" and \"west coast\" (CA). Story text adds RI and CA. Fine.\n\nFor SJW, \"High prevalence ... in Vermont, New Hampshire, Washington, and Oregon.\" Compute late 2016: WY, VT, AK, DE, OR, ME, NH, WA,... VT, OR, NH, WA top (excluding WY/AK/DE/ME). Close. Maybe specific 4-week window.\n\nFor shook, \"Why California and Hawaii?\" — late 2016/summer, CA and HI top. Our data: summer CA #2 behind HI; Sep-Dec CA #1, HI #2. Good.\n\nFor bough, \"MISSISSIPPI\" — late 2016 MS #1; 2016 average MS #1.\n\nFor boolin, \"Boolin emerged from the south\" — perhaps early period (2015) southern states. Our data at 2015 average: MS an